===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*mew-}}
The {{glossary|adjective}} is derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|muddi}}, {{m|enm|moddy}}, {{m|enm|muddy|t=covered with or full of mud, muddy}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=muddī|pos=adj|id=MED28855}}</ref> from {{m|enm|mud}}, {{m|enm|mudde|t=mud; turbid water}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=mud(de|pos=n|id=MED28854}}</ref> + {{m|enm|-i|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|adjective}}s}}.<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=-ī̆|pos=suf|id=MED21577}}</ref> {{m|enm||Mud}}, {{m|enm||mudde}} is possibly borrowed from {{der|en|dum|modde}}, and/or {{der|en|gml|modde}}, {{m|gml|mudde}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*mud-}}, {{m|gem-pro|*mudra-|t=mud}}, possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro|*mū-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*mew-|t=moist}}. The English word is analysable as {{suffix|en|mud|y|pos2=suffix meaning ‘having the quality of’ forming adjectives}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''adj.'' and ''n.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=123257|date=September 2022|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref> {{doublet|en|muddle}}.

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the adjective.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=123258|date=March 2022|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

{{rel-top|cognates}}
* {{cog|gml|moddich}}, {{m|gml|muddich}} ({{cog|nds-de|muddig|t=muddy; mouldy}})
{{rel-bottom}}

